    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Project KO11 Is Proof That Geely Wants To \"Knock Out\" Range Rover, Launches Next Year in Europe\nFIA Prez Ben Sulayem Claims F1 Cars Will Go V8 Next Decade\n8 photos\nPhoto: Max Verstappen on Facebook\nFormula 1 will race with V8 power units again from the 2030 season, FIA president Mohammed Ben Sulayem announced today in the capital of Rwanda, on a stage built for the launch of the African Grand Prix. Regarding the latter announcement, a 10-year agreement returns the sport to the African continent for the first time since the 1993 edition of the South African Grand Prix.\nOn a panel with Formula 1 chief exec Stefano Domenicali and Rwandan prez Paul Kagame, the big kahuna at the Federation Internationale de l'Automobile said that Rwanda will hear the new V8s in the early 2030s. Putting an emphasis on 2030s because it's not clear if the Bugesera circuit near Kigali will be ready in 2030.\nSulayem kept its V8 confirmation relatively short, giving it a 2030 launch date and a venue where it will race in the 2030s. There is, however, no rulebook to speak of as of this writing. A vote on the V8 era hasn't been held either, and don't even dare ask about specifications every manufacturer has to abide by.\nFor the time being, it's generally agreed that a displacement of 3.0 liters is on the menu. There is also talk about a turbocharger and a much smaller electric side of the powertrain. If these details prove on point, look forward to a vastly different V8 era from the 2.4-liter naturally aspirated recipe from 2006.\nA naturally aspirated V8 would blow past the noise limits of several current Formula 1 venues, with street circuits being the biggest problem for the new eighter. Given the aforementioned, a turbocharger would help the 3.0-liter mill absorb part of the sound as the turbine sits in the exhaust flow.\nPhoto: Max Verstappen on Facebook Electrification, meanwhile, has been unpopular with the manufacturers and fans alike. From 2030 onward, electrification could survive with a 100-kW boost rather than a 50:50 split for today's V6 turbo power units. At the end of the day, however, nothing past V8 and 2030 has been confirmed by Sulayem to date.\nGiven that the current regulation cycle should run through the end of 2030 , teams are extremely likely to be asked if they're fine with ending the 50:50 turbo V6 hybrid era in 2029. Speaking from the standpoint of a motorsport lover with some rallying experience to his name, the FIA president said that going V8 would make the next generation of cars approximately 80 kilograms (176 pounds) lighter.\nA different objective of the new era is 50 percent less research and development on the powertrain side, which is a tremendous improvement from the current V6 power units. Speaking of, Ben Sulayem made a clear distinction between the sounds F1 enthusiasts want and the boring noises they do not. Sustainable fuels are yet another piece of the puzzle, though it's not clear what kind of green fuels are planned for the new eighter.\nStandardization is certain to play a big role in the new era, and there's also talk about a push-to-pass mode. A potential issue with the V8 era is that certain teams won't be happy about ditching the V6 so quickly after entering the sport. Those outfits are, of course, Cadillac and Audi. View this post on Instagram A post shared by Mohammed Ben Sulayem (@mohammed.ben.sulayem)\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nF1 Formula 1 v8 regulations fia\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Owner Passed Before Finishing This 1961 Ford, the Mileage Is Unexpected",
